    "turn_text": "would race up the pole and come down, turn the camera off, and I'd log roll for them and talk about kind of what I'm doing in law school and my family and set the tape in. So that's the next phase and so I went to like a 10-12 step interview process and was selected to be part of MTV's The Real World, Boston. I had been going to school for a long time, and I just thought it would be an interesting break. And not sure that I was going to get it, but I thought it wouldn't be fun to go have this experience. And for me, being born and raised in a small town, I was going to law school in Minneapolis. I was doing lumberjack competitions and exhibitions, you know, at the prime of my competing years. So I thought it would be a unique, life-forming experience, so I thought I'd try it out. This concept is you take seven complete strangers from all different walks of life and force them to live in a house together for six months. The shows were basically a week of airing, a week of filming 24 hours a day, seven days a week, and that would be edited down to 22 minutes. And this is a process where every aspect of your life is filmed and recorded, so you don't have any moments that aren't on camera. And what I found interesting was when I got there, I was living on a TV set. My kitchen was a TV set, my bedroom was a TV set, my living room was a TV set. That's a weird environment to walk into. But how humans adapt? after three weeks or four weeks. This was my house. This was my home and it became pretty normal that I would have crew members walking around filming and biking and you adapt and this is my environment. This is my home and you become very natural in a really odd setting. And what's unique about the show is you can't communicate with the cameraman or the sound people. So you see them every day. You might have an impression with who they are, but they're prohibited from talking to you and you can't talk to them. And at the end of the show, we have a cast and crew party where you actually get to talk to these people who you've practically lived with for six months, but whenever we'll talk to. And you know that they had opinions and views about all the fights, all the conflict. And when they first started the concept, they thought they had to create drama in conflict in the house. What they soon realized was, if you put people together in a house that are very different, you will get drama, you will get conflict, and you'll get a great storyline. Because in life, we surround ourselves with people who are like us, who think like us, that believe like us, oftentimes that look like us. But for me, it was probably one of the best experiences because As I look at my life and my life experiences, I never would have come into contact with the people that I was forced to live with on that show. And what I realized was that after spending six months with them, the differences that I thought we had at the start faded away and I found that I had much more in common with them than I would have believed at first blush. I think that's true in life. If you actually spend time to get to know someone, you realize there's a huge lane of issues and ideas that you share in common. But what we do is we go to the things that separate us first, as opposed to the things that bring us together. When I finished the real world, I'd taken my semester off of law school. About a week after I got done with the real world that came and said, hey, we're going to do this new show. It's going to have the real world cast members from each of the seasons do road rules where you do this travel adventure show. And I had to go to the dean of the law school, ask for another semester off, which he approved. and signed up for it. It was a three-week travel adventure show, which I thought would just be kind of like the real world. What I soon found out was that the last person I met coming off the train of all my cast mates was my wife. She got off the train, she handed me her bag. I'd say that I've been carrying her bag ever since then. But we had a great experience together. There were also two very different people. I mean, she was a military brat, southeast, Latina, Spanish speaking. Not really the type of person, type of woman I'd meet in northern Wisconsin. We spent three weeks together, cultivated a friendship. I ended up falling in love and dating and getting married, and I'll claim to fame is that we're the first reality TV couple that met on her show and got married. We now have seven kids. Our eighth is on the way, and I'm happy living in Walsaw, Wisconsin. The lesson for me in this is, keep people's chance. Don't always take what you might think is the first blush of who someone is. For me, we're looking at the house. That has been a huge benefit to realize that first glance isn't always the right glance. I mean, this is a sampling and a slice of all of America. With a wide range of cultures and viewpoints, they come together for this Rockis American conversation, and we are at a critical point in American history. This is one of those points where We're going to determine the future, not just of this generation, but future generations, the path that we go down. And it's a conversation of really big ideas. And if we want our ideas to win, which I think are the ideas of hope and opportunity and prosperity and American strength, we have to be able to reach people no matter where they're at. We don't have to go through MTV or ABC or NBC or CNN to get a message out. It's our job to reach out and bring the message that we have that is a traditional American message that has made us so strong and so prosperous and so free.",
